boot-steam.gif

What it is
  The startup animation of the Steam Machine, which the panel plays once
  while it joins the network.

Where it came from
  Valve Corporation. It is their work and their marks, and this project
  holds no licence to it. It is here because the owner of this repository
  put it here for their own Steam Machine and decided to carry it.

  It is NOT under the GPL-3.0-or-later of this project, and no part of this
  repository grants anybody a right to it. Anybody who forks this repository
  and wants this file has the same question to answer that we did.

What was done to it
  The source was a 1920x1080 VP9 clip at 30 frames each second, 3.117
  seconds long. All of its content sits in a 600x600 square in the middle of
  the frame and the rest is black, so that square was cut out, put on
  320x320, and written as a GIF of 64 colours at 30 frames each second. The
  sound was dropped. Nothing else was changed.

  The steps, for anybody who wants to make it again:

    ffmpeg -i <source> -vf "crop=600:600:660:240,scale=320:320:flags=lanczos,\
    fps=30,split[a][b];[a]palettegen=max_colors=64[p];[b][p]paletteuse=\
    dither=none" -gifflags -transdiff -loop 0 boot-steam.gif

  -gifflags -transdiff is not decoration, and the first image shipped
  without it played on the board as a square of solid green. Three things
  meet to make that:

    ffmpeg writes each frame as only the pixels that changed and marks the
    rest transparent, which is the ordinary way to keep a GIF small. All 94
    frames of the first image were written that way.

    ffmpeg pads the palette of 64 colours out to 256, sets the background
    colour of the image to index 255, and entry 255 is #00FF00.

    LVGL, in gif_blend_to_rgb565, writes that background colour over every
    transparent pixel instead of leaving the pixel of the frame before. Its
    own comment says "to maintain position". The path for ARGB8888 does not
    do this, which is why a preview at 32 bits showed a correct screen.

  With the flag every frame is whole and nothing is transparent, so the
  background colour is never written anywhere. The file is 2 KB smaller
  either way, because the picture is mostly flat black.

  check_boot renders this image at sixteen bits and fails on a green pixel,
  so a later re-encode cannot bring the fault back in silence.

How to take it out
  Delete this file and boot-steam.gif. The panel then starts without an
  animation. See firmware/companion/main/panel_boot.c, which does nothing
  at all when it gets no image.
